47:A number of approaches are put forward to resolve the morphological heterogeneity of gray matter in neuropsychiatric disorders using objective MRI data. Normative model can infer individualized neuroimaging differences of a patient by measuring its deviates from the healthy distribution (Marquand et al. 2016). Using this method, researchers successfully quantify structural brain heterogeneity at subject level in mental disorders (Wolfers et al. 2018; Zabihi et al. 2019; Wolfers and Beckmann 2020). For example, using the normative model, Wolfers et al. find that the individualized gray matter morphological differences in schizophrenia and bipolar disorder would be concealed by traditional case-control designs, providing a step toward a systematic mapping of the heterogeneity of mental disorders (Wolfers et al. 2018). The similar conclusions are found in attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (Wolfers and Beckmann 2020) and autism spectrum disorder (Zabihi et al. 2019). Individualized neuroimaging differences further help to identify more homogeneous subtypes with distinct (even opposite) neuroanatomical differences profiles (Liu et al. 2021; Shan et al. 2022) and clinical manifestations, reconciling the conflicting findings of neuroimaging studies and facilitating personalized clinical decision-making in mental disorders (Han, Xu, Guo, Fang, Wei, et al. 2022a, 2022b, 2022e; Han, Zheng, et al. 2022; Shan et al. 2022). However, these pioneer studies do not provide effective methods to deal with the high-dimensional results derived from the normative model (Wolfers et al. 2018; Wolfers and Beckmann 2020). Additionally, the quantitative association between subjectlevel differences derived from the normative model and grouplevel differences are not well elucidated. In other words, previous studies cannot explain how subject-level differences contribute to the observed group-level differences, leading to a lack of interpretability of subject-level differences.
51:(Ashburner and Friston 2000) and parsed them into latent disease factors using non-negative matrix factorization (NMF). As a promising method widely used in MRI studies, NMF decomposed high-dimensional data into a factor matrix (latent disease factors) and a weight matrix (factor compositions) (Anderson et al. 2014; Shan et al. 2022). Thus, individualized morphological differences were represented as a linear combination of the identified disease factors. The framework assigned each patient a unique factor composition, thus retaining interindividual variability. We posited that each patient with OCD expressed multiple latent disease factors rather than one single factor. In this study, we would show that factor compositions were associated with clinical characteristics capturing sources of clinical heterogeneity. We further showed that our framework could significantly infer factor compositions of unseen patients. What' more, we demonstrated that group-level morphological differences could be quantitatively derived from the identified disease factors, reconciling the group-level and individualized findings in neuroimaging studies. Finally, we uncovered two putative OCD subtypes with opposite morphological differences compared with HCs from factor compositions.
53:## Methods
95:Then, we identified the potential disease factors from individualized morphological differences using non-negative matrix factorization (NMF). As a data-driven multivariate method, NMF produced a sparse, parts-based representation of the data under non-negativity constraints (Lee and Seung 1999). Compared with independent component analysis (ICA) and principal component analysis (PCA), the results of NMF were more explanatory (Sotiras et al. 2017). In addition, NMF yielded a soft (probabilistic) parcellation of voxel-wise aberrance and was proved to accurately identify subject-specific functional networks (Li et al. 2017) and reveal individualized coordinated patterns of cortical development during adolescence (Sotiras et al. 2017 be expressed as the following formula:
149:embedded patients with OCD (2 positive and 2 negative weights) into two-dimensional space using t -distributed stochastic neighbor embedding method (hexagrams in Fig. 4C) (Laurens and Hinton 2008).
171:The coefficients of the regression model were drawn in Fig. 4B. In this study, the group-level differences were posited to be an 'average' patient where the coefficients of the regression model were treated as its factor compositions. We also embedded the 'average' patient into two-dimensional space using t -distributed stochastic neighbor embedding method (red circle Fig. 4C). As we could see, the group-level differences were not representative of most patients.
203:level and these was no effective method to handle with the results (Wolfers et al. 2018; Wolfers and Beckmann 2020). In this study, we first obtained individualized morphological abnormalities using the normative model and went further to parse them into latent disease factors corresponding to distinct differential patterns in OCDusing NMF.In the context of the notion that OCD was a spectrum disorder, we posited that one patient with OCD more than one latent disease factors rather than single one. The interindividual heterogeneity among patients was embodied in the various factor compositions. This notion was consistent with the dimensional model of psychiatric disorders in the diagnostic and statistical manual of mental disorders, fifth edition (DSM-5) (Chen et al. 2022). Most of previous studies resolved the heterogeneity by dividing patients with OCD into non-overlapping/categorical subtypes according to neuropsychological measures, clinical manifestations, and neuroimaging representations (Beijers et al. 2019). Despite the potential benefits, they encountered numbers of problems such as selection of clustering algorithm, determination of the optimal number of subtypes and more serious was that some patients might not clearly belonged to any subtype (van Hulst et al. 2015; Marquand et al. 2016). Although similar methods were proposed to uncover disease factors in Alzheimer's disease (Zhang et al. 2016) and autism spectrum disorder (Shan et al. 2022), they did not capture individualized differences or obtain disease-related differential patterns.
225:Although a number of methods inferring personalized differences were proposed to resolve the heterogeneity, the derived subject-level differences were hard to understand (Marquand et al. 2016; Han, Xu, Guo, Fang, Wei, et al. 2022a, 2022b, 2022e; Han, Zheng , et al. 2022). In this study, we showed that group-level differences were only representative of the 'average' patient (red circle in Fig. 4C). What's more, our framework derived more explanatory individualized differences by showing that group-level differences could be quantitatively derived from the identified disease factors, reconciling the group-level and subjectlevel findings in neuroimaging studies.
261:- Ashburner J, Friston KJ. Voxel-based morphometry-the methods. Neuroimage . 2000:11:805-821.
